   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network carries a video stream from the internet to a laptop through a series of steps involving the router, access points, radio channels, and packets. When you start streaming a video on your laptop, the request for the video content is sent to the internet. This request travels through the internet, eventually reaching a server that hosts the video content.\n\nThe server sends the video data back to your laptop in small chunks called packets. These packets are transmitted over the air using radio waves, which are managed by the router and the access point in your home.\n\nThe router acts as the central hub of your home network. It connects to the internet via a cable or fiber optic connection and then distributes the internet signal to the access point. The access point, which is often built into the router or a separate device, broadcasts the Wi-Fi signal that your laptop uses to connect to the internet.\n\nThe Wi-Fi signal operates on specific radio channels. In the United States, there are 13 non-overlapping channels in the 2.4 GHz band, and 14 in the 5 GHz band. When you connect your laptop to the Wi-Fi network, it selects a channel to use for communication. If multiple devices are transmitting on the same channel, they can interfere"
